Generate CLI configuration commands that fulfill the user's request using the information from the reference content. Please respond directly to the user's question. Do not mention phrases like "based on the information you provided", "according to the documentation", or anything similar.
To configure a Nokia router to tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status, use the following CLI commands:

```bash
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            bfd-enable
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

Alternatively, you can enable BFD at the group level to apply it to all neighbors in the group:

```bash
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        bfd-enable
        neighbor <ip-address>
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

Ensure BFD is properly configured on the interface(s) used for the BGP session(s) before enabling this feature.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
"How to configure a Nokia router to tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure a Nokia router to tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status, use the following CLI commands:

```bash
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            bfd-enable
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

Alternatively, you can enable BFD at the group level to apply it to all neighbors in the group:

```bash
